### Baud Rate & Bit Time Physics

**Baud rate** is the number of signal transitions (bits) transmitted per second.

$$\text{Bit Duration } (T) = \frac{1}{\text{Baud Rate}}$$

At **9600 baud**:
$$T = \frac{1}{9600} \approx 0.000104166\text{ seconds} = 104.17\ \mu\text{s}$$

Because each byte requires 10 total bits (1 Start + 8 Data + 1 Stop):
$$\text{Effective Throughput} = \frac{9600\text{ bits/s}}{10\text{ bits/byte}} = 960\text{ bytes/second}$$

```cpp
// Monster Lab - UART Frame Timing Analyzer
const unsigned long BAUD = 9600;
const unsigned long BIT_TIME_US = 1000000UL / BAUD; // ~104 microseconds
const int BITS_PER_FRAME = 10; // 1 Start + 8 Data + 1 Stop

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(BAUD);
  while (!Serial) { ; }

  Serial.println("--- UART 8-N-1 TIMING BENCHMARK ---");
  Serial.print("Configured Baud Rate: ");
  Serial.print(BAUD);
  Serial.println(" bits/sec");

  Serial.print("Single Bit Duration: ");
  Serial.print(BIT_TIME_US);
  Serial.println(" microseconds (us)");

  Serial.print("Theoretical Max Throughput: ");
  Serial.print(BAUD / BITS_PER_FRAME);
  Serial.println(" bytes/sec");
}

void loop() {
  // Transmit 10 bytes and measure elapsed time
  unsigned long tStart = micros();
  Serial.print("1234567890");
  Serial.flush(); // Wait until hardware transmission finishes
  unsigned long tElapsed = micros() - tStart;

  Serial.println();
  Serial.print("Actual 10-byte transmit duration: ");
  Serial.print(tElapsed);
  Serial.println(" us");
  delay(3000);
}
```

### Code Breakdown:
- `1000000UL / BAUD` calculates microsecond bit time with integer math.
- `Serial.flush()` halts execution until all buffered outgoing bits physically leave the ATmega hardware shift register.
